// XFAIL: RT-291: `{:5?}` and `{:<4?}` of a char that Debug writes as `\u{..}` put the padding inside the braces: `'\u{   7f}'`, `'\u{7f  }'`, `'\u{ 200b}'`; rustc's Debug of a char takes no width at all (`'\u{7f}'`, `'\n'`, `'a'` as they are) (0.30.2)
// rustc prints the EXPECT lines (twin .rs.txt); the release build
// `['\u{   7f}']`, `['\u{7f  }']`, `['\u{ 200b}']`, `['\u{         7f}']`,
// then `['\n']` and `['a']` (right); v0.28.0 to v0.30.1 print `[' {   7f}']`
// (the `\u` lost as well). `{:10?}` of a str with a `\t` and `{:?}` of the
// same chars without a width are right (r302f fm/f1.vl: 795 integer, str, char and bool specs
// against rustc; the only other differences are RT-16's `{:x?}` and RT-13's
// '\u{10ffff}').
// EXPECT: ['\u{7f}']
// EXPECT: ['\u{7f}']
// EXPECT: ['\u{200b}']
// EXPECT: ['\u{7f}']
// EXPECT: ['\n']
// EXPECT: ['a']
fn main() {
    println("[{:5?}]", '\u{7f}');
    println("[{:<4?}]", '\u{7f}');
    println("[{:5?}]", '\u{200b}');
    println("[{:>11?}]", '\u{7f}');
    println("[{:5?}]", '\n');
    println("[{:5?}]", 'a');
}
